nse intense incentives  and I must admit I'm not convinced that  the unemployed sons spending 17 hours in  the bay  smit of his mother's house working on  his grand theft auto 5 is making a grand  contribution but tax incentives are  making a grand contribution to gamers  bottom line and it's all thanks in part  to the nature of the video game industry  which extends across the realms of  software development entertainment and  online retail making it possible to  combine tax breaks in ways other  entertainment businesses cannot years  before the first video game was even  released Congress rewrote the tax code  to allow companies to deduct all  lab-based research and development in  1969 this was expanded to allow  organizations to deduct the cost of  software development another research  and design credit was added in 1981  after fears that Japan's dominance in  the auto industry would lead to a  technological decline in the United  States these federal breaks in addition  to state incentives have been a major  boon for the gaming industry even when  they're conducting research so it has  little value outside of existing games  the old expression was throwing bales of  money out of the helicopter and that's  largely what these things are there is  no application of any human intelligence  as to no necessity and the slightest bit  that there has to be general proved  nobody there bargaining in the stingy  hard-hearted way of I will pay for this  because it's worth it don't get me wrong  if gamers are willing to pay for it  that's the free market but there isn't  any reason why we should double the rate  of return for those gamers because then  we start producing games that people  don't really want that much we're  wasting stuff because things through  twice as cheap as they ought to be twice  as profitable as they thought of me the  market ought to decide these things  while Johnson maintains that gaming  subsidies do more harm than good  supporters of the program say that  incentives are necessary to keep America  competitive in the global economy  without these incentives they argue we  could see gaming jobs outsourced to  nations with friendlier corporate tax  rates but several studies have called  into question the effectiveness of these  programs a 2013 analysis done by the Tax  Foundation found that film tax  incentives only generated 30 cents in  tax  for every dollar spent the government  shouldn't be the one who determines  winners and losers it should be the real  demand of people who really want this  stuff and while Texas is spending more  on gaming incentives more states like  Kansas Missouri and Connecticut are  scaling back or eliminating their  programs altogether perhaps legislators  are learning that states benefit when  they have a tax policy that is welcoming  to all industries instead of subsidizing  some businesses at the expense of others  you
 

